// `let x = e` and `let S { .. } = e` are one statement form with two
// spellings, and only one had a tail. The destructuring branch ate a `;` and
// returned, so two statements written on one line raised no [S-Separator] and
// the program was accepted -- while its twin, `let q: P = p m = q.x`, is
// refused.
//
// The same gap silenced [S-Continuation]: a next line beginning with `-` could
// have continued the expression above, and the note that says so is what the
// formatter reads to decide whether a written `;` is meaningful. Without it a
// separator answering nothing was kept.
struct P {
    x: UInt8,
    y: UInt8,
}

fn main() -> UInt8 {
    let p: P = .{ x = 1, y = 2 }
    var m: UInt8 = 0
    let P { x: a, y: b } = p m = a
    m + b
}
